WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.860
(dinging and music box playing)

00:00:01.860 --> 00:00:05.800
- Entropy is the gradual
decline into disorder.

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:07.289
I'm always aware of it passing

00:00:07.289 --> 00:00:10.076
and the toll that it's taking
on all the objects around me.

00:00:10.076 --> 00:00:11.093
(bird singing)

00:00:11.093 --> 00:00:13.916
These objects need someone to make sure

00:00:13.916 --> 00:00:16.460
that, 100 years from now, they're
gonna be delighting people

00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:18.891
just as they are today.

00:00:18.891 --> 00:00:22.989
(dramatic orchestral music)

00:00:22.989 --> 00:00:25.355
I'm an antiquarian horologist.

00:00:25.355 --> 00:00:29.522
It's quite a rare title
to have next to your name.

00:00:31.201 --> 00:00:35.368
I work on objects that have
a watch or clock mechanism.

00:00:37.055 --> 00:00:39.239
I'm maybe one of two
people that has a master's

00:00:39.239 --> 00:00:42.906
in horological conservation
in this country.

00:00:44.118 --> 00:00:46.015
I'm basically a mechanic,

00:00:46.015 --> 00:00:47.921
but a mechanic from the 17th century.

00:00:47.921 --> 00:00:50.187
(watch ticking)

00:00:50.187 --> 00:00:53.229
I specialize in micromechanics,

00:00:53.229 --> 00:00:55.506
and I work on machines called automata.

00:00:55.506 --> 00:00:56.937
(winding clicking)

00:00:56.937 --> 00:00:59.973
They're made to mimic
living things in the world.

00:00:59.973 --> 00:01:03.371
Human beings, birds, animals, flowers.

00:01:03.371 --> 00:01:05.734
I think of them as the first instances

00:01:05.734 --> 00:01:08.354
of artificial intelligence.

00:01:08.354 --> 00:01:11.271
(winding clicking)

00:01:12.587 --> 00:01:15.170
(bird singing)

00:01:18.545 --> 00:01:21.713
Everything is self-contained
in these mechanisms.

00:01:21.713 --> 00:01:23.904
They run usually with springs.

00:01:23.904 --> 00:01:26.186
So it's not running on batteries.

00:01:26.186 --> 00:01:28.608
It's all running from
just brass and steel.

00:01:28.608 --> 00:01:31.287
(serene instrumental music)

00:01:31.287 --> 00:01:34.277
Any time I get an object that's broken,

00:01:34.277 --> 00:01:37.777
there's this innate need to bring it back.

00:01:39.419 --> 00:01:41.412
Because these objects are so old,

00:01:41.412 --> 00:01:42.866
if I need a part that I don't have,

00:01:42.866 --> 00:01:45.092
which is more often than not the case,

00:01:45.092 --> 00:01:47.509
I will go and make it myself.

00:01:49.337 --> 00:01:51.938
(ratcheting)

00:01:51.938 --> 00:01:56.880
It's the closest you can get
to bringing something to life.

00:01:56.880 --> 00:01:58.603
You get a silent bird.

00:01:58.603 --> 00:02:00.446
The wings are broken.

00:02:00.446 --> 00:02:01.392
There's no feathers.

00:02:01.392 --> 00:02:02.868
It doesn't have eyes.

00:02:02.868 --> 00:02:05.274
I get to bring all those things back,

00:02:05.274 --> 00:02:06.844
so that it can sing again.

00:02:06.844 --> 00:02:09.427
(bird singing)

00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:15.225
(dramatic orchestral music)

00:02:15.225 --> 00:02:18.948
I feel they're a significant
part of our cultural heritage.

00:02:18.948 --> 00:02:23.016
They were made in ways that I
couldn't even do myself.

00:02:23.016 --> 00:02:24.108
They were done by candlelight.

00:02:24.108 --> 00:02:25.272
They didn't have electricity,

00:02:25.272 --> 00:02:29.439
so I know they were using
a foot pedal to cut wheels.

00:02:30.996 --> 00:02:33.864
It's an incredible amount
of work and craftsmanship

00:02:33.864 --> 00:02:36.642
just to create something
beautiful for the sake of that.

00:02:36.642 --> 00:02:38.751
(bird singing)

00:02:38.751 --> 00:02:41.503
People believed that these
horologists were sorcerers,

00:02:41.503 --> 00:02:45.670
so in a way I guess I'm a
modern antique sorcerer.

